From 587de95d32d25d1c336f94c820622afb019da50c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 08:22:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 131/172] fixup! media: amlogic: amvdec: add VP9 decoding

Tidy the platform format tables:
The GXBB, GXL, GXLX and GXM tables build their H.264 entry from a shared
macro while every other entry is spelled out per SoC, the G12A and SM1
tables list their codecs in a different order from the GX tables, and
the tables carry comments that restate their contents.

Write the H.264 entry out in each GX table and drop the macro, sort the
codec entries of every table by pixel format, and drop the comments
within the tables. Each table keeps the same entries.

Limit GXBB HEVC decoding to 1080p:
4K HEVC playback on GXBB is not stable: the HDMI output repeatedly loses
sync, where GXL is stable with the same media.

Limit GXBB HEVC to 1920x1088, as its H.264 and MPEG-2 decoding already
is, so players fall back to another decoder for 4K content.

Decode H.264 up to 4K on GXL and GXM:
The GXL and GXM H.264 formats cap the coded size at 1920x1088 and the
level at 4.2, so 4K H.264 streams are rejected and fall back to
software decoding. Both SoCs decode H.264 up to 3840x2160 at 30fps on a
single decoder core, and the workspace and motion vector buffers are
sized from the stream.

Raise the GXL and GXM H.264 limits to 3840x2160 and level 5.1, as on
G12A and SM1. GXBB stays limited to 1080p.

Accept HEVC coded sizes up to 4096x2304:
The HEVC formats on GXL, GXLX, GXM, G12A and SM1 cap the coded size
at 3840x2160. Encoders may code UHD content as 3840x2176 and crop it
back to 3840x2160, and those streams are rejected before decoding
starts even though the workspace, motion vector buffer and frame MMU
map are all sized for 4096x2304.

Raise the HEVC limit to 4096x2304.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
